# Tests of the Keycloak-protected IRIS APIs - run them from VS Code with the REST Client extension
# (humao.rest-client): click "Send Request" above each request. No terminal needed.
# Secrets are read from keycloak/.env (a link to keycloak/secrets.env, created by ./keycloak/init-secrets.sh).
# Two targets in this file: LOCAL (sections 0 to 5, Docker on this machine) and AWS (sections A0 to A5, below).
# AWS needs AWS_IRIS_SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET in keycloak/secrets.env (client secret of iris-service on the AWS Keycloak:
# ssh to the host and read IRIS_SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET in intersystems-iris-health-training/keycloak/secrets.env).
# Every IRIS request carries "# @no-cookie-jar": REST Client would otherwise send back the IRIS session cookie opened by
# request 2, and request 4 ("without credential") would silently be authenticated.
# Prerequisites: the "keycloak" name in /etc/hosts (127.0.0.1 keycloak) and the containers started.
# See docs/Keycloak-IRIS-Security.md, sections 4.1 and 4.2.

@keycloak = http://keycloak:8180
# Port 80 keeps these tests free of the self-signed certificate. For HTTPS use https://localhost
# (REST Client must then accept the local certificate: setting "http.proxyStrictSSL": false).
@iris = http://localhost
@namespace = dglab

### 0.a Keycloak is up and the realm "iris" exists
# Expected: 200, "issuer":"http://keycloak:8180/realms/iris", and S256 in code_challenge_methods_supported
GET {{keycloak}}/realms/iris/.well-known/openid-configuration

### 0.b nginx serves the web application
# Expected: 200
GET http://localhost/app/

### 0.c The portal over HTTPS, without session, redirects to the Keycloak login
# Expected: 302 with a Location header "http://keycloak:8180/realms/iris/protocol/openid-connect/auth?...code_challenge_method=S256..."
# Needs HTTPS: if REST Client refuses the local self-signed certificate, set "http.proxyStrictSSL": false in the VS Code settings.
# @no-redirect
GET https://localhost/iris-health-training-dev/csp/sys/UtilHome.csp

### 1. Get an access token (client_credentials, client iris-service)
# Expected: 200 and a JSON body with "access_token"; the token's issuer is http://keycloak:8180/realms/iris
# @name login
POST {{keycloak}}/realms/iris/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=iris-service&client_secret={{$dotenv IRIS_SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET}}

###
@token = {{login.response.body.$.access_token}}

### 2. HL7 LAB-27 with a VALID token
# Expected: 200 and an RSP^K11 answer (MSA|AA, QAK|Q1|OK): the request is authenticated by the Keycloak token
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{iris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/healthshare/{{namespace}}/EnsLib.HL7.Service.HTTPService.cls?CfgItem=LAW%20LAB-27%20Query%20for%20AWOS%20-%20HTTP
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: text/plain

MSH|^~\&|ANALYZER|LAB|LIS|LAB|20261113190000||QBP^Q11^QBP_Q11|QBP1|P|2.5.1|||NE|AL||UNICODE UTF-8|||LAB-27^IHE
QPD|WOS^Work Order Step^IHE_LABTF|Q1|CT99
RCP|I||R

### 3. The same request with a TAMPERED token (a character is appended to the signature)
# Expected: 200 but an HTML "Login IRIS" page, no RSP^K11: the signature is rejected.
# (Do not "tamper" by replacing the last character: it carries only 2 useful bits, so some replacements give the same signature.)
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{iris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/healthshare/{{namespace}}/EnsLib.HL7.Service.HTTPService.cls?CfgItem=LAW%20LAB-27%20Query%20for%20AWOS%20-%20HTTP
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}x
Content-Type: text/plain

MSH|^~\&|ANALYZER|LAB|LIS|LAB|20261113190000||QBP^Q11^QBP_Q11|QBP1|P|2.5.1|||NE|AL||UNICODE UTF-8|||LAB-27^IHE
QPD|WOS^Work Order Step^IHE_LABTF|Q1|CT99
RCP|I||R

### 4. The same request WITHOUT any credential
# Expected: 200 but an HTML "Login IRIS" page, no RSP^K11
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{iris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/healthshare/{{namespace}}/EnsLib.HL7.Service.HTTPService.cls?CfgItem=LAW%20LAB-27%20Query%20for%20AWOS%20-%20HTTP
Content-Type: text/plain

MSH|^~\&|ANALYZER|LAB|LIS|LAB|20261113190000||QBP^Q11^QBP_Q11|QBP1|P|2.5.1|||NE|AL||UNICODE UTF-8|||LAB-27^IHE
QPD|WOS^Work Order Step^IHE_LABTF|Q1|CT99
RCP|I||R

### 5. Portal page with the machine token (this account holds no Keycloak role, so no IRIS role)
# Expected: 403 "An error occurred with the web application": the account is authenticated, but not authorized.
# To see the authorization at work, grant a realm role (for example iris-admin) to the service account of the client
# iris-service in Keycloak, get a new token (request 1) and send this request again: the page then opens.
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{iris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/sys/sec/%25CSP.UI.Portal.Users.zen
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

################################################################################
### AWS  (https://<public dns> : Keycloak under /auth, nginx on 443)
################################################################################
# The AWS certificate is self-signed (Let's Encrypt refuses EC2 names): REST Client must accept it, for example with the
# VS Code setting "http.proxyStrictSSL": false. The AWS Keycloak admin console is NOT public (404): SSH tunnel, see the docs.

@awsHost = ec2-63-177-72-122.eu-central-1.compute.amazonaws.com
@awsKeycloak = https://{{awsHost}}/auth
@awsIris = https://{{awsHost}}

### A0.a AWS Keycloak is up and the realm "iris" exists
# Expected: 200 and "issuer":"https://<public dns>/auth/realms/iris" (the public URL, also found in the tokens)
GET {{awsKeycloak}}/realms/iris/.well-known/openid-configuration

### A0.b The Keycloak admin console is NOT public
# Expected: 404 (the admin console and the master realm are only reachable through an SSH tunnel)
GET {{awsKeycloak}}/admin/master/console/

### A0.c The portal, without session, redirects to the Keycloak login
# Expected: 302 with a Location header "https://<public dns>/auth/realms/iris/protocol/openid-connect/auth?...code_challenge_method=S256..."
# @no-redirect
GET {{awsIris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/sys/UtilHome.csp

### A1. Get an access token from the AWS Keycloak (client_credentials, client iris-service)
# Expected: 200 and a JSON body with "access_token"; the token's issuer is https://<public dns>/auth/realms/iris
# @name awsLogin
POST {{awsKeycloak}}/realms/iris/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=iris-service&client_secret={{$dotenv AWS_IRIS_SERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET}}

###
@awsToken = {{awsLogin.response.body.$.access_token}}

### A2. HL7 LAB-27 on AWS with a VALID token
# Expected: 200 and an RSP^K11 answer (MSA|AA, QAK|Q1|OK)
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{awsIris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/healthshare/{{namespace}}/EnsLib.HL7.Service.HTTPService.cls?CfgItem=LAW%20LAB-27%20Query%20for%20AWOS%20-%20HTTP
Authorization: Bearer {{awsToken}}
Content-Type: text/plain

MSH|^~\&|ANALYZER|LAB|LIS|LAB|20261113190000||QBP^Q11^QBP_Q11|QBP1|P|2.5.1|||NE|AL||UNICODE UTF-8|||LAB-27^IHE
QPD|WOS^Work Order Step^IHE_LABTF|Q1|CT99
RCP|I||R

### A3. The same request on AWS with a TAMPERED token (a character is appended to the signature)
# Expected: 200 but an HTML "Login IRIS" page, no RSP^K11
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{awsIris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/healthshare/{{namespace}}/EnsLib.HL7.Service.HTTPService.cls?CfgItem=LAW%20LAB-27%20Query%20for%20AWOS%20-%20HTTP
Authorization: Bearer {{awsToken}}x
Content-Type: text/plain

MSH|^~\&|ANALYZER|LAB|LIS|LAB|20261113190000||QBP^Q11^QBP_Q11|QBP1|P|2.5.1|||NE|AL||UNICODE UTF-8|||LAB-27^IHE
QPD|WOS^Work Order Step^IHE_LABTF|Q1|CT99
RCP|I||R

### A4. The same request on AWS WITHOUT any credential
# Expected: 200 but an HTML "Login IRIS" page, no RSP^K11
# @no-cookie-jar
POST {{awsIris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/healthshare/{{namespace}}/EnsLib.HL7.Service.HTTPService.cls?CfgItem=LAW%20LAB-27%20Query%20for%20AWOS%20-%20HTTP
Content-Type: text/plain

MSH|^~\&|ANALYZER|LAB|LIS|LAB|20261113190000||QBP^Q11^QBP_Q11|QBP1|P|2.5.1|||NE|AL||UNICODE UTF-8|||LAB-27^IHE
QPD|WOS^Work Order Step^IHE_LABTF|Q1|CT99
RCP|I||R

### A5. The portal on AWS with a machine token alone
# Expected: 302 (to the Keycloak login). On AWS the portal sits behind oauth2-proxy: a bearer token does not replace the
# browser login (unlike the local port 80, where the same request answers 403 for an account without role).
# @no-redirect
# @no-cookie-jar
GET {{awsIris}}/iris-health-training-dev/csp/sys/sec/%25CSP.UI.Portal.Users.zen
Authorization: Bearer {{awsToken}}
